examples.solution_text:
a. The gradient is $-400$ litres per hour: the volume decreases by $400$ L each hour. The vertical intercept is $2400$ L, the initial volume.

b. $\begin{aligned} V &= 2400 - 400(2.5) \\ &= 2400 - 1000 \\ &= 1400 \end{aligned}$

The predicted volume is $1400$ L.

c. $\begin{aligned} 0 &= 2400 - 400t \\ 400t &= 2400 \\ t &= 6 \end{aligned}$

The model predicts an empty tank after $6$ hours.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=0.018mm]
\definecolor{modelblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (0,0)--(7,0);
\draw[->,black] (0,0)--(0,2800);
\draw[thick,modelblue] (0,2400)--(6,0);
\fill[modelblue] (0,2400) circle (1.2pt);
\fill[modelblue] (6,0) circle (1.2pt);
\path (0,2400) node[left,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] {$2400$};
\path (6,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] {$6$};
\path (3.5,-550) node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] {Time (h)};
\path (-1.6,1400) node[rotate=90,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] {Volume (L)};
\path (4.8,650) node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] {Empty at $6$ h};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

d. $\begin{aligned} V &= 2400 - 400(8) \\ &= 2400 - 3200 \\ &= -800 \end{aligned}$

A volume of $-800$ L is impossible. The draining model applies only until the tank is empty, for $0 \le t \le 6$. After that, the volume cannot keep decreasing at this rate.
